    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. One thing we're really worried about is the \"unexplained\" delay factor. Sometimes, even when the flow looks okay on paper, there are these random spikes in congestion or small bursts of chaos where the throughput just drops for no obvious reason—like a sudden bottleneck that doesn't align with the volume. How do these two designs typically handle those kinds of unpredictable interruptions or sudden increases in systemic friction?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Unpredictable delays, or what you're calling \"bursts of chaos,\" are often where the rigid nature of signalized intersections shows its weakness. A signalized junction, when faced with an unexpected surge, is stuck in its programmed cycle, which can turn a minor hiccup into a major, systemic blockage. The roundabout, being more adaptive, tends to absorb these sudden surges more gracefully, as drivers are constantly yielding and merging rather than stopping entirely. It's less prone to the sudden, catastrophic failure mode that a fixed-time signal can experience when its capacity is unexpectedly exceeded.",
      "source": "target",
